<h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2><p>Every year, Bangladeshi universities produce thousands of final-year projects, theses and hackathon prototypes. Some of them answer questions that ministries and agencies are actively asking. Almost none reach those ministries. The route from a student’s laptop to a government file does not exist in any formal sense.</p>
<p>Where ideas do travel, they travel through personal networks. That is unfair to the innovator, who has no protection and no credit, and it is risky for government, which has no way to judge the evidence behind what it receives.</p>
<blockquote>The question is not whether young Bangladeshis have ideas. It is whether the state has a door they can walk through.</blockquote>
<h2 id="what-a-fair-pathway-needs">What a fair pathway needs</h2><p>A working pathway needs three things. First, a public record that shows who had an idea and when, so credit is never in doubt. Second, an independent validation step, so decision-makers receive evidence rather than enthusiasm. Third, a clear adoption rule, so a ministry knows how to pilot an idea and what it owes the person who had it.</p>
<h2 id="our-recommendation">Our recommendation</h2><p>We propose a five-step pathway, from intake to implementation, that a ministry or a2i could adopt without new legislation. The full brief sets out each step, the roles involved and a simple credit and benefit-sharing rule.</p>
